WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.980
Welcome to Academy
Dialogues, It Starts With Us.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.530
My name is Shawn Finnie.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.109
We are continuing our ongoing
series of conversations

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:10.960
about race, ethnicity,
history, opportunity,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.928
and the art of filmmaking.

00:00:12.928 --> 00:00:14.470
Today's discussion
group will explore

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:16.780
the invisibility of
Latinos in Hollywood,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.700
the complexity of
Latin identity,

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.710
legacies of colonialism,
and solutions

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.930
to advance representation.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.780
Moderating today's
conversation is Lorenza Muñoz.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.110
EVP, Member Relations and
Awards at the Academy of Motion

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.540
Pictures Arts and Sciences.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.680
Victoria Alonso, EVP
Production Marvel Studios.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.800
Casting Director
Producer Carmen Cuba.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.170
Director, Nadia Hallgren.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.810
Actress, producer,
director, and businesswoman

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:48.040
Eva Longoria, and marketing
strategist Yvette Rodriguez.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.470
We look forward to your
conversation today.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:51.550
Hello, everybody.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.240
Welcome  It's so
wonderful to have you all here.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.370
It's such a dream for
me to be able to host

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.150
this conversation that
is absolutely necessary

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.140
and I think we're all going to
really enjoy and commune over.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.610
So without much further
ado, let's get started.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.240
So tell us, what brought
you to Hollywood?

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.070
What was your story?

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.440
And what have you
faced along the way?

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.120
Let's start with Eva.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.830
Of course, you start
with me as my son

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.770
is marching through the room.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.960
Mama loves you.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.850
So yeah, my gosh my journey
has been a long one.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.130
I never wanted to be an actor.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:35.713
I didn't even know
about the industry.

00:01:35.713 --> 00:01:38.390
I grew up in Corpus
Christi, Texas.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.300
And we had a TV.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.640
We didn't even go to the
movies because it was just

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.630
too expensive.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.400
We would go to
the dollar cinemas

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.470
which were the movies
that were out already

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.270
for a very long time.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.020
So that was the closest as I
got to experience in Hollywood.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.320
But I don't know what happened.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.340
I went to college and one day I
said, I'm going to be an actor.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.470
And I told my mom,
I said, I think

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.180
I'm going to become an actor and
move to LA with $200 in my bank

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.420
account and went and got a job.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:07.360
I just figured it out.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:13.750
I figured out I think my
journey to today, which is more

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.180
directing and producing.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.430
People who think I'm an actor
turned director-producer

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.190
but I always tell people, I
was always a director producer

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.200
who happened to fall
into acting as a means

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.750
to the end of
becoming a director.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.930
But for me it's just been--

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.680
it's been a fun journey
because I remember

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.860
arriving in Hollywood and
not being Latina enough,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.500
because I didn't speak Spanish
and I didn't have an accent.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.240
And every audition I
went on they were like,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.780
could you do it more
like Rosie Perez?

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.590
And I was like, well, I don't--

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:48.700
I'm not from New York.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.090
I'm not Puerto Rico, and
I got a specific dialect.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.000
And then-- and I
didn't speak Spanish.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.427
I couldn't go out in Spanish
language things either.

00:02:57.427 --> 00:02:59.760
So I wasn't Latin enough and
then I wasn't white enough,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.000
and so I got Italian roles.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.410
I was Eva Longoria.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.630
I was like, great, I'll take it.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.600
I will do that role
for a long time.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.140
And what it made me realize
in the industry was obviously

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.720
stereotypes and what people
think a Latina should sound

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.220
like or look like.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.510
But also, that Hollywood
thought we were monolithic.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.040
That there's not Afro-Latinos.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.910
That we're all of one sexuality.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:33.268
That we are all supposed to be
gorgeous and sexy and feisty.

00:03:33.268 --> 00:03:34.810
I remember I kept
getting a note when

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.000
I was on Young and the Restless,
could you do it more Latina?

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.690
And I'm like, I am Latina.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:45.160
Like I can't do it any more
Latina because I am Latina.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.840
And so there was a big learning
curve for our industry.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.670
And I think that
today people always

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:52.840
go, how are we doing today?

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.710
And I said, we're facing
the right direction now.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.270
I don't know we've taken enough
steps in the right direction,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.060
but at least we're facing
the right direction

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.852
and we're having these
conversations, which

00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:04.602
is really important.

00:04:04.602 --> 00:04:05.560
That's wonderful, yeah.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.720
And as you can see from
our beautiful panel

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.420
how different we all are
and we are all Latinos.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.600
It's wonderful our diversity.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.270
Victoria Alonso.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.310
Gosh, I don't know.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:22.310
I was never supposed
to be in Hollywood.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.500
I grew up in Argentina,
born and raised.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:32.030
I'm a teenager of the military
dictatorship where I always say

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.770
that the theater saved my life.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:36.380
So-- because instead
of being in marches

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.460
I was at the theater
playing, rehearsing,

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.980
and I was very involved
in the marches.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.930
So I often have told
the story of how

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.120
I used to hold the
banners in the front row.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.010
I know that's a surprise.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:55.310
And because the banners
were so heavy we ended up--

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.480
I ended up like staying
back two or three rows.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.558
But when they shot, they
shot at the first row

00:05:01.558 --> 00:05:02.350
and the second row.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:05.090
And by the time that the
third row was about to be hit

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.590
we had a chance to run.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:13.070
So all that to say that when
I wanted to study something

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:16.430
I studied theater and
psychology because that's

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.760
what my father was.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.630
And that was a chance for
me to get close to him

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.150
and he died at the age of 40.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.280
So he died young when
I was 

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.380
When I came to The
States I had never

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.910
thought that I would
be in production.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:33.500
And I had-- in LA
I have three jobs.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:37.250
And I worked at Alaska
Airlines at 5:00

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.950
in the morning, 5:00 to
12:00 for a long time.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.150
And then I will go to Paramount
Pictures to be a page.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.093
And I did all the live shows.

00:05:46.093 --> 00:05:47.510
And then now during
the weekends I

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.610
would have a Black
Angus waitress job.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.700
This is with-- I had a full
college education already

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.880
finished, but I was trying to
figure out what I was doing.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.452
And I tell you the
Alaska Airlines story

00:05:58.452 --> 00:05:59.660
because I think is important.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:00.890
A friend of mine
at Alaska Airlines

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:02.970
said, hey, I think you'll
be great in production.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.650
And I'm like, what is
production because I was doing--

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:10.100
I was helping
produce a show called

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.100
Frida Kahlo at the Bilingual
Foundation of the Arts.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.820
And she said, I
have a there that

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.700
works in production you
should meet with her.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:25.180
So I got to meet with Carla and
she gives me my first PA job.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.740
That to say from there on
that was almost 30 years ago.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.760
But when we did Captain Marvel
I worked at Alaska Airlines

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.240
and I didn't want to
ask my mother for money.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:41.140
So I had the three jobs and
I had jobs that gave me food.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:45.280
Alaska Airlines gave me the
leftover food from first class

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.770
because they threw it away, I
ate it daily and I took a home.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.440
Then I would go to the shows,
the live shows at Paramount.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:54.580
They fed all of us.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:55.690
That was beautiful.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:56.710
I ate at night.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:57.720
And on the weekends--

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.770
I'm Argentinian
no denying there.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:05.020
I ate steak at Black
Angus for a reduced price.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.590
When we did Captain
Marvel, Alaska airlines

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:12.830
had a couple of planes
with Captain Marvel on it.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:17.680
And I remember being in a
marketing meeting and I saw it,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.000
and I just that--

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.100
I said nothing, I was just--
my tears were coming down.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.550
What full circle to those days?

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.950
Look, I'm talking about
a plane and here is

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:32.590
the Santa Monica Airport
gives us the plane q sound.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:33.140
I love it.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.150
This is a great time.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.860
I remember thinking if you would
have written it there's-- they

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:42.390
would have said, no
that's a little too much.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.280
That you go from--

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:49.920
that you go from eating the food
and working at Alaska Airlines

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:52.110
to having eat--

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.680
their plane having our film.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.660
That was one of the pinnacles
alongside with Black Panther.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.960
I always say this,
pinnacles of my career.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.690
So from production
from PA to now

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.780
Alongside with Louis
Kevin heading the studio,

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.400
I'm on my 30th Marvel
film and my eight--

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.990
as in eighth streaming show
and second animation show.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.670
So we-- anyway, long
story to tell you there.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:29.340
Amazing trajectory and game
changing, what you've done.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.980
Nadia Hallgren, tell
us about your journey.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:33.480
Wow.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:34.620
So my journey--

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.530
I still don't feel Hollywood.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.450
I feel like documentary.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.840
I never imagined being
a documentary filmmaker

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.755
would land me somehow
anywhere near Hollywood.

00:08:46.755 --> 00:08:48.690
I'm born and raised
in South Bronx

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.870
and I started to be
interested in documentary

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.380
through a community
photography program

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.000
that my mom encouraged me to go
to at the International Center

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.310
Photography.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:02.340
And it just really
stirred up this interest

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:05.670
in the storytelling and
gave me an opportunity

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.240
to try and make sense of
the world that I love them.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:12.750
And then I started
making short films

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.800
and through the biggest
stroke of luck in my life

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.100
I met one of Michael
Moore's producers

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:24.420
at a community film
festival in the Bronx.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:25.895
And she gave me--

00:09:25.895 --> 00:09:27.270
her name Tia
Lessin-- she gave me

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.437
my very first job
as a PA as well,

00:09:29.437 --> 00:09:31.770
which is, I always think those
are great stories working

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.630
on Fahrenheit 9/11.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:37.770
And at that point,
I decided that I

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.810
wanted to be a cinematographer.

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:44.400
I felt like being DP
gave me an opportunity

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.380
to not have to speak.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.990
I could show up on set
I could be quiet and not

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.440
have to expose my--

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:53.370
what I thought was
my lack of education

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.870
or that I came from the hood.

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.130
Before I worked in film
I was a nightclub bouncer

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:09.840
and I worked my way up
started working as a DP

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.650
and then ultimately started
directing documentaries,

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.330
and that's why I'm here.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:15.830
Awesome.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.450
I love that you were a bouncer.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:20.130
So cool.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:20.750
You know me.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:23.135
So you can probably
now imagine that.

00:10:23.135 --> 00:10:25.118
Sure there's a thousands
stories that we

00:10:25.118 --> 00:10:26.410
can hear from you even though--

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.610
Yeah, so we could just do
the whole thing on that.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:29.730
Oh, my God.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.150
Listen, that's a good
documentary too Nadia.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.370
It is.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.900
I love it.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.065
Carmen Cuba, tell us your story.

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:48.810
Yeah, well, like most of us
so far this was not my plan.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:55.260
My father is Peruvian and was a
political economist for the UN

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:55.810
growing up.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.040
And my mom is American of
Dutch and Italian descent.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:03.180
And was a Spanish teacher
who started her connection

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:07.590
to Latin-America at 15 when
she was an exchange student.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:10.050
She was a-- she had
skipped various grades

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.840
and she was an exchange
student college in Colombia.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.230
So my two parents met
both being fluent speaker.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:20.370
Well, my father was
not a fluent American--

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:23.040
English speaker but my
mother spoke Spanish fluently

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.660
and they met in college
and had me and I

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.820
lived between Peru and
Bolivia very, very immersed.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.510
Peru Bolivia and
eventually New Mexico,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:41.130
but I was very immersed
in a world that

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:46.080
was surrounded by people from
all over the world from birth.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:51.700
Many of my parent's colleagues
were from all over the world.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:55.420
I went to an international
school in Bolivia.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.560
So my first friends
were African.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:01.290
And anyway that's just
to say that at the stage

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:05.490
was set for me to explore and
be very interested in humans

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.310
from all different backgrounds,
from myself and have--

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:11.700
I come from-- a great
respect for that idea

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.470
of authentic diversity in the
lives that we actually live.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:20.310
And when I moved to
the states I moved to--

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:22.380
when I was around 11--

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.850
I moved to New Mexico, which
is another very complicated

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.610
ethnic mix.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.860
And there my mom
got me involved in--

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:34.920
like Nadia your mom--
my mom got me involved

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.920
in a bilingual theater group.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.390
And it was one of only three
in the country at the time.

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:49.170
And it was run by an openly gay,
Puerto Rican, HIV positive man.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:52.050
And so that was my--
and he was in New Mexico

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:56.820
trying to tell these stories
with non-professional actors.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:02.640
He was trying to start a real
community theater company.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:07.620
A lot of it focusing on who are
new Mexicans and who are we.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:12.840
So in that, I ran lights, I
did sound, I was on stage,

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.210
I was one of only
two kids because it

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:16.680
was all-- it was really adults.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.260
So I came up through that.

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:22.650
All the while thinking
I needed to have

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.410
a real job when I grew up.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:30.510
So I went into journalism and
I studied journalism and then

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:34.470
from there I was a news
reporter on Univision.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.870
Back when there were only
three channels in New Mexico

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.760
and only one of them
was Spanish speaking.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.450
So I did that and
then I came to--

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:46.120
but actually when I was
doing that I realized that I

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.010
love people and I love stories.

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:51.620
And doing news you only got
sort of, this accident happened.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:52.660
Here's the person.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.150
Who, what, where, when, why.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.040
And I always wanted to follow
those people home and a month

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:01.270
later and be like, so
how did it affect you?

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:03.430
And so I really--

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:05.740
always have really connected
to these human stories.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.030
So I came to LA
because a boyfriend

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.220
that I had in
college lived here.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:17.020
And I pursued--

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:20.770
I thought I was going to
pursue documentary film.

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:23.560
And one thing led to someone--

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.350
like Victoria said-- someone
suggested I meet someone.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:29.650
And suddenly I was in
post-production on the second

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:33.740
season of The Real World, which
is shooting in Los Angeles,

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:36.280
which at the time was--

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.810
it was a true documentary.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:39.280
It wasn't reality TV.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.300
It was really about the people.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:44.560
And anyway, so then I
worked my way through that

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:48.400
and they suggested I try casting
since I was a journalist.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:51.290
And they didn't-- no one knew
what casting was back then

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:51.790
of that.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:56.740
So I did that for a while
and loved it and I--

00:14:56.740 --> 00:15:00.670
even back then I had they did a
spinoff show called Road Rules

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:04.450
and it was about this group of
kids traveling across America.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:05.740
And to me because the--

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:10.210
I also lived on a Navajo
reservation for one

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:12.100
of the years that I was--

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:13.490
after I moved to New Mexico.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:18.040
And so I put the first
Native-American person on MTV

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:18.940
in the '90s.

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:20.950
And to me that was--
just made total sense

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:27.670
because it was the first show of
kids traveling across America.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.520
So anyway, it's always
been part of my life.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:32.680
And from there
when things became

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:36.820
reality TV somebody suggested
that I try narrative

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:42.910
and I said yes and that's
how I am here today.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:43.600
Wonderful.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.090
Wow, that's amazing.

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.550
I was an exchange student too.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:48.740
Oh, wow.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.500
In San Diego.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.580
And I showed up in--

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:54.650
I come from the
dictatorship, right,

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:56.630
uniforms you can do
all these things.

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:58.670
And here are all
these girls and boys

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:01.160
in their swimming
suits and flip flops

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.220
and they don't have a
lot of clothes in school.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.232
And I was appalled.

00:16:05.232 --> 00:16:07.190
I was like, they're going
to come and get them.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:08.900
This is not going to go well.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.790
And it took me like
three months to get it

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:13.640
that they were not in trouble.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:16.490
Anyway, we digress.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:22.010
It's funny how all
of our cultural stuff

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:23.510
that comes with us, right?

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:25.250
I'm from Mexico.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.650
And when I came
here in the summer

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:32.960
when I was six years old, and
in Mexico a lot of people go,

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:36.170
ti-ti-ti oh, yeah,
oh, yeah, ti-ti right?

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:39.230
And the camp
counselor looked to me

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:43.060
and she was like, in
America we do that.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:45.590
I'm not a dog.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:48.650
So that was my first
foray into, oh, OK.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:51.660
This is what I do
now in Mexico yes.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:52.160
Yeah.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.170
So if somebody
says of the you go,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:57.200
and so I do this in
meetings now people go,

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:59.830
do you have a question, Eva?

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:03.780
And I go, no, no I'm
agreeing with you.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:06.170
Why?

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:09.829
Oh, and Ivette,
Ivette Rodriguez.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:10.940
Tell us your story.

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:11.710
My story.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:16.670
Hi, well, I was born in Puerto
Rico and I moved to New Jersey

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.579
to a small town in
New Jersey 45 minutes

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:21.680
from the city with my parents.

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.650
Actually on American airlines
with all the Rodriguezs

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:26.569
we all moved to this
little small town.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:32.540
And I was raised-- it was very
much a working class town.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.000
And I was-- I grew
up with Polish people

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.240
and Italian, African-American
like everybody.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.250
Worked for
Johnson&amp;Johnson or Ford.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.040
So we all owned homes.

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:43.400
So the American dream, right?

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.730
That's what I grew up
in and then go to--

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:50.240
but definitely no understanding
that there was an industry.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.270
Like I didn't understand there
was a film industry or record

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:54.380
industry.

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:55.880
The best thing you
can think of is,

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:57.700
you're going to become
a doctor or a lawyer

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:00.560
that this was what
you aspired to do.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.200
And I was in New York
City going to school

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:06.170
and working as a
paralegal at a law firm.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:09.610
When my name-- when my
roommate came home and told me

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:11.360
that she had just gone
for a job interview

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.120
with Arista Records
and she was appalled

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:15.710
because they weren't pay there.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:18.170
I don't even remember
what the amount was

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:20.840
but it was not a high paying
job and she had already

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:22.220
graduated college.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.500
So then I decided
that I would go,

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:26.030
that I would like
the opportunity

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:28.640
to experience a job interview.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:32.430
And I went for a job interview
at 6 West 57th Street.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.760
And when I got off
on the seventh floor,

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:38.570
I saw all these gold and
platinum records on the wall.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:42.740
And I was like, oh, my God
where am I. I had no idea.

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.900
Growing up loving
film, loving music

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.360
but not being
sophisticated enough

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.410
to know that there
was an industry.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.460
So at that point, I met with
my-- who became my mentor.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:53.730
I interviewed with her.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:56.360
And at the end of the interview,
I extended my hand to her

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.140
and I said thank you for
your time I can start Monday.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:02.600
And she said, OK,
we'll see you then.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.990
And that was the beginning of my
career at Arista Records Clive

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:07.730
Davis.

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:10.580
First artist was
Whitney Houston.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.250
But immediately I
noticed that I was--

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:14.870
I didn't know I was
different actually

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:16.480
until I started working.

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:18.800
And because people wanted
my opinion about things

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:21.410
that I didn't understand why
because I was a Jersey girl,

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:23.300
rock and roll girl like why?

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.160
And it was because of
the color of my skin.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:26.720
I quickly understood.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:30.620
And so got to Orion
pictures, which is

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.300
why this is really full circle.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:33.890
They just announced
what they do next

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.520
but Orion pictures was nearby
and I could see the posters

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:38.270
on the wall.

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:40.913
When you walk-- I would walk
for lunch and I would look up

00:19:40.913 --> 00:19:42.830
and there's were movie
posters I was like, oh,

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.360
my God what is this?

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:46.700
And then, we worked
on the soundtrack

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:48.590
for Dying Young, Julia Roberts.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.600
I was like a big
fan of Julia Roberts

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.100
and we get to work
on the soundtrack

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:55.400
because Kenny G did
the main theme song.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:58.070
And I went to my
first screening,

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:00.170
right, to meet with the
publicity department

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:01.460
from the studio.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:04.880
And I was like oh, my
God what is this, right?

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.560
And so I moved West.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.530
I moved West to get
into the film industry.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:15.590
And I started and I was in
the rock, in the studio.

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:20.043
And I quickly understood
that I was different.

00:20:20.043 --> 00:20:22.460
Just being in meetings really
being the only brown person.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:23.280
I'm aging myself.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.775
But this is a really
long time ago.

00:20:24.775 --> 00:20:26.150
We've come a long
way but nowhere

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.350
near where we need to be.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:34.240
But I remember a feeling when
the studio executive, the head

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:38.380
of a studio came
to me too excited

00:20:38.380 --> 00:20:40.510
to let me know that
he now understood

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:42.460
my culture because he
just got done seeing

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:45.050
a film about a Puerto
Rican in the south,

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.410
and like a poverty-stricken
Puerto Rican in the South

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:48.400
Bronx.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.778
And he says, I really
understand your culture now.

00:20:50.778 --> 00:20:51.820
And this is a true story.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:54.640
I was like, oh, OK.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:57.125
I mean, it was just like shock.

00:20:57.125 --> 00:20:58.250
I didn't even say anything.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:20:59.290
And people that
know me will say,

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:00.957
I don't believe you
didn't say anything.

00:21:00.957 --> 00:21:02.110
I didn't say anything.

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:04.040
I found my voice later in life.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:07.130
I was just shocked but that
has happened a lot to me.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:11.050
And so I think I was always
a little activist at heart.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:14.470
And actually my experience--

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:17.410
both of these experiences
in records and in film

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:18.950
actually propelled my career.

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:21.760
So I started my
own company in 1997

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:24.460
specifically called it
American entertainment mark.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:28.640
Because I was like,
hello, we are American

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:31.720
and so it was just
to like authentically

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:34.240
connect with the
Latino consumer.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.700
And-- so it's interesting
that Hollywood

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:38.830
has pushed where-- this
industry has pushed

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:40.780
me to start my own company.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:43.430
And now push me to want
to produce my own films.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:46.000
So this is a--

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:47.110
that's my story I hope.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.270
Is that my full story?

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:52.030
So what movie did
you see that dude?

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.570
Oh, you're going to ask me that?

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:56.070
Now we're going
to know who it is?

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.812
I bet we can guess if
she tells us the year.

00:22:00.812 --> 00:22:03.270
Because there was one movie
every five years, maybe, right?

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.650
Is a Puerto Rican movie.

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:06.570
Was it West Side Story?

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:07.200
No.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:08.230
Was it Selena?

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:09.650
I Like It Like That.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:13.510
Selena was Mexican-American.

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.960
I Like It Like That.

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.640
Oh, I Like It Like That.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.810
Sorry, charades.

00:22:21.810 --> 00:22:25.020
Well, that leads us to
the next question, which

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.550
is about stereotypes, right?

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:31.100
And how-- I think Ivette
also touched on this as well,

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:34.290
is this just this
general perception of us.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:38.750
That's either you're feisty,
you're angry, you're fiery,

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:42.090
what a temper, you're
so sexy, you're so hot,

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:45.120
you're so-- you're the
maid, you're the 

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:46.020
you're the--

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:48.240
like there are all
of these stereotypes

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:52.410
that we all-- have
all had to deal with.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:57.590
Tell us some of your stories
and we could start with Nadia.

00:22:57.590 --> 00:22:58.090
Wow.

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:06.560
I think, for me
especially I came up

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.350
and I still am just
behind the camera.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.900
As a cinematographer and someone
who like the technical side,

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:17.020
I think the main experience
that I had that was always

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:19.180
a little bit crushing was--

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.010
the minute I walked
in the room people

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:23.410
had no idea what
I was doing there.

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:26.650
It would be typically
a crew looked

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:30.110
like a roomful of white men.

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:34.360
And when I walked in it was
like, what are you doing here?

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:36.040
And it was this
up and down look.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:37.990
A lot at my hair.

00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:41.080
I would try to even
consider if that day I

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:43.330
was going to work if
it was very humid out.

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:46.780
How I can might not bring
so much attention to myself.

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:51.160
But that was something
very common that happened.

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:52.540
And even though
it's a look, it's

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:57.280
just this feeling that then sits
with you an entire day of work.

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:01.990
And I think something
that surprised--

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:04.180
I guess surprised me and
it shouldn't have was

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:09.880
I thought as I moved up
in experience and credits

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:11.990
that I would get
treated differently

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:18.280
but the amount of times that I'm
just completely underestimated

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:23.650
in my job and my
ability at times is--

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:27.580
Yeah, I think it just
speaks to systemically

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:35.410
this stereotype that is rampant
in all industries by just about

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:41.140
where we come from or an
intellect, our abilities.

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:43.620
And I think that that's
just-- that's definitely,

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:46.470
probably one of the
things that have--

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:49.360
I've had to work within
myself and within the people

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:52.260
that I collaborate with
the most to try and create

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:55.200
an environment that makes
it possible to show up

00:24:55.200 --> 00:25:01.630
to work every day and be happy
and satisfied with the process.

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:02.400
Yes.

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.995
It's so funny, because I
think that is something--

00:25:04.995 --> 00:25:06.870
we have the double whammy
because we're women

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:08.400
and we're people of color.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:10.500
And dimming our light
when we go on a set,

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:13.410
especially as a director, I
remember I had a guy tell me

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:15.990
after I-- we finished
directing episodes, I mean,

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:19.350
he goes you know
you you're good.

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:20.245
And I was like--

00:25:20.245 --> 00:25:22.050
What a joke.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:23.340
Thank you.

00:25:23.340 --> 00:25:25.110
He assumed weren't.

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:27.900
Yeah, he expected
me not to be good.

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:31.035
And so that dimming your
light and like I just don't--

00:25:31.035 --> 00:25:35.590
I want to be small and not
have anybody notice me.

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:37.530
And I was directing
while breastfeeding.

00:25:37.530 --> 00:25:38.910
I had just had my baby.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:40.860
And I said, oh, my God
I don't want the guys--

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:42.480
the 90% of the crew--

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.230
to feel, I didn't want
them to feel uncomfortable.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.408
That's-- I was worried
about them feeling as--

00:25:47.408 --> 00:25:49.950
I was like, I'm going to have
to take breaks to pump and then

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:51.600
to breastfeed, da, da, da.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.700
And it's like dimming our light.

00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:54.360
For who?

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:54.860
Why?

00:25:57.210 --> 00:25:59.450
Be your best and let
everybody deal with it.

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:02.338
But I've I felt that
way, too, Nadie.

00:26:02.338 --> 00:26:04.380
You're like, ah, should
I wear my hair different.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:09.070
I always thinking about
what to wear to set,

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:10.858
because I want to
be comfortable but I

00:26:10.858 --> 00:26:12.150
don't want to look like a slob.

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:14.820
And then I'm a girl, but
I don't want to be girly.

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:17.880
And it's like, it's so much you
have to navigate because you

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:19.865
want to be seen as a boss.

00:26:19.865 --> 00:26:21.990
And it's too much to think
about before you're even

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.430
doing the work.

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:25.710
That's not hard enough.

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:28.710
It's like we don't even know
what they feel because they've

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:32.640
never had to have that little
chapter of the book, which

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.140
we do.

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:36.990
I don't know that I've
been discriminated.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.960
I am pretty loud
and feisty and I

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:43.980
tend to advocate to the
good stereotypes of who

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:45.270
we are as passionate women.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:48.600
But I tell the story--

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:52.380
I would say, halfway into our
successful career at Marvel

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:56.373
came this man that came
to pitch as a director.

00:26:56.373 --> 00:26:57.540
I've told this story before.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:59.500
It's the story of the coffee.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:03.150
And when you're about
to be interviewed

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:06.600
at Marvel, if you're
one of the two people

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:09.720
that were trying to
decide who is who,

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:12.450
that you get to meet
Louis, Kevin, and myself.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:15.720
And usually there's the
other creative producer.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:17.910
There's four of us that
will walk it through.

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.740
And you've met with
the other person,

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:21.240
but you haven't met
the three of us.

00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:23.750
We were running so
late for this meeting.

00:27:23.750 --> 00:27:26.070
And the other, the
fourth producer

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:29.460
had gone already to say, I'm
sorry, they're running late.

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:30.720
I'm sorry, the guys--

00:27:30.720 --> 00:27:33.460
because we're always the
guys, and I'm a part of that.

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:35.460
And I'm OK with it.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.140
The guys are running behind.

00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:39.930
So finally I leave the meeting
that I'm with Kevin and Louis

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.430
and I said, you
know what, one of us

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.820
needs to show 
We're way behind.

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:46.260
I'll go and I'll tell.

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:47.370
When I get there--

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:50.640
and I said gosh, I'm so sorry.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:53.500
We're running really behind.

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:55.950
Have they even asked you--
do you want anything?

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:57.990
Can I get you anything to drink?

00:27:57.990 --> 00:27:59.100
I want coffee.

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:00.240
I want coffee with milk.

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:02.760
And I want it now.

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:04.460
I said, oh.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:07.865
And I thought, wow,
you are nervous.

00:28:07.865 --> 00:28:12.470
And I'm thinking, OK,
well, let's walk over here.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:15.440
The conference room was here and
there was a little coffee area

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:17.990
there.

00:28:17.990 --> 00:28:20.840
But a true, true jerk.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.630
And so I'm making the
coffee, and I'm thinking--

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:24.920
because I'm Argentinian.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:27.440
So I battle my ego all day.

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:28.790
You're making the coffee?

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:30.233
You're making the coffee?

00:28:30.233 --> 00:28:32.620
Wait, I'm not done, Longoria.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:33.180
Pará.

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:34.610
Pará.

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:36.050
So I go in.

00:28:36.050 --> 00:28:38.300
I make the coffee,
and I put in Equal.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:39.470
And I don't put milk.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:41.410
He wanted sugar and milk.

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:45.570
And he goes, god dammit, I
told you I did not want--

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:48.590
and I said, no, no, no,
I heard what you wanted.

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:49.610
This is my coffee.

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:55.640
Now I walked you through how
the coffee machine works.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.260
You saw me do it.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.540
I'm sure you were
quick to learn.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:01.940
I will be right
back with the guys.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:04.280
So enjoy your coffee.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:05.760
And I walked out.

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.850
And that was that
moment where you can

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:10.900
let the ego be alive and well.

00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:12.870
I could have said
all kinds of things.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.312
I could have said
all kinds of things

00:29:15.312 --> 00:29:17.520
because, to be honest with
you, they were in my head.

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:20.170
They were all, [SOUNDS].

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:22.480
And I was, shh,

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:27.420
And I thought, you
know what, I won't

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:31.720
forget this moment I'm sure
he won't forget this moment.

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:33.690
And so I walked away.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:38.090
Three of us came in,
and he was purple,

00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:39.350
purple when I walked in.

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:41.480
He was like, oh my god.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.650
Now needless to say, he pitched.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:46.992
It wasn't a good pitch, and
he didn't get the job, which

00:29:46.992 --> 00:29:48.200
is always what they asked me.

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:49.300
Did he get the job?

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.717
But it had nothing to do with
the fact that he was a jerk.

00:29:51.717 --> 00:29:53.480
The pitch was not a good pitch.

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:57.890
But even at that moment in time
where we had made $10 billion

00:29:57.890 --> 00:30:01.790
worth of successful films,
we had had the first Avenger

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:02.870
movies out.

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:04.095
We've had many movies.

00:30:04.095 --> 00:30:04.970
I mean, I don't know.

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:06.980
I think we were
on movie number--

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.650
we're movie 30 now, which is
a different kind of success.

00:30:09.650 --> 00:30:12.350
But at that moment
in time, on movie--

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:15.080
whatever-- 10 that
we were or eight,

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:19.040
we had the kind of success
that people talk about because,

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:21.050
if you have one movie
that makes a lot of money,

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:23.000
people talk about it.

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:25.430
But even at that moment
in time, the assumption

00:30:25.430 --> 00:30:29.720
was that I was the girl,
that I would have taken notes

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:31.070
or I would have made him coffee.

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:37.150
Carmen, tell us your stories.

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:39.490
Well, it's interesting
listening to all of that

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:41.020
because I think--

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:46.510
because I have Peruvian
and American in my family

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:51.280
and in my experience,
I sort of grew up--

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:55.180
my mother's family's
from Queens.

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:58.840
And my grandfather, who
I was very close to,

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.990
was an NYPD lieutenant.

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:06.550
And the story was
I was his favorite.

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:10.960
And yet when he was introducing
me to people, he would say,

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:15.165
this is my Puerto
Rican granddaughter.

00:31:15.165 --> 00:31:16.540
But he didn't know
the difference

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:19.390
between Puerto Rico and Peru.

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:22.130
It's like in the early '70s.

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:27.250
And my mom, his daughter, was so
mortified and mad all the time.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:31.050
And I was like, it
doesn't matter to me.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:32.490
Like, I love him.

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:33.360
He loves me.

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:34.800
It's fine.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:37.270
So I think growing
up, feeling like,

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:43.470
oh, people just don't
know set the stage for me

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:47.220
because I don't really
notice it in a way

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:52.740
because I have been able to
be successful despite whatever

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:55.555
bias has come my way.

00:31:55.555 --> 00:31:57.180
Lorenza, we talked
about this a little.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:04.090
I have the privilege of ethnic
ambiguity, which is a thing,

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:04.960
too.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:09.110
It's like people, they
mostly think I'm Asian.

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:13.600
So that experience is, I
don't know what people think.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:16.300
But I know that they don't
know what I actually am most

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:17.740
of the time until they ask.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:21.760
And so I do think I've
had privilege in that way.

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:24.850
And I think all of us, we have
these different privileges, as

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:28.420
well as these biases against
us, or if you're tall,

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:31.730
if you look a certain way.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:37.150
So I would say that
my ethnic ambiguity

00:32:37.150 --> 00:32:42.010
has actually given me a
lot of permission to make

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:45.850
a lot of moves inside the
dominant culture of Hollywood

00:32:45.850 --> 00:32:50.380
and inside the mainstream movies
that I've worked on because I

00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:54.460
can suggest something that
someone else would not

00:32:54.460 --> 00:32:55.330
have suggested.

00:32:55.330 --> 00:32:59.590
And I have actually gotten
mostly very positive response.

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:04.090
Like, when I say,
oh, in Bowfinger,

00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:06.480
couldn't that
shopkeeper be Asian.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:10.690
And then I present them John
Cho, and they go, oh, yeah.

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:14.070
But if I wasn't there and
I hadn't presented that,

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:16.540
it would have been white
because that's just the default.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:19.510
And certainly back then,
it's much better now.

00:33:19.510 --> 00:33:22.720
But anyway, I don't know if
that answers the question.

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:25.120
No, it's so funny because
I had that privilege, too,

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:26.830
because I didn't have an accent.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:29.920
People assumed many
times that I was white.

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:32.770
And so they would be
like Latino, that Latino.

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:35.890
And I'm like, well,
I'm one of them.

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:36.460
Don't forget.

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:38.280
And they would forget.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:42.580
For me, to talk about
stereotypes specifically--

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:43.630
because I'm listening--

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:44.950
I feel like we all have
the same experience.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:47.080
I'm like, have I been
discriminated against because

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:47.920
of my ethnicity.

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:51.010
I think we've all
experienced a lot of sexism.

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:54.860
And I've spoken to a lot
of women and of color,

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:56.893
and they always go,
what's the cross you bear.

00:33:56.893 --> 00:33:58.810
And they always go, oh,
definitely because I'm

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:02.290
a woman, not so much because
I'm a Latina woman, but a woman.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:04.150
But stereotypes in the media--

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:08.692
so if you're talking
about storytelling,

00:34:08.692 --> 00:34:10.150
there's two sides
of the coin and I

00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:14.563
have two opinions about
it because I produced

00:34:14.563 --> 00:34:15.730
a show called Devious Maids.

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:17.530
I produced and directed it.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:21.340
And the backlash,
because I was Latina

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:26.230
doing a show about
maids, was like, wow,

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:28.860
why are you stereotyping
us again, whatever.

00:34:28.860 --> 00:34:31.300
And if you saw the
show, the maids

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:35.445
were the moral
compass of the story.

00:34:35.445 --> 00:34:37.570
And the employers were the
assholes and the idiots.

00:34:37.570 --> 00:34:41.380
We were making a point about
how you treat domestic workers.

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:45.820
And I also took offense that
you think those people don't

00:34:45.820 --> 00:34:47.409
have stories to tell--

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:50.469
gardeners, domestic
workers, nannies, maids.

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:52.929
You're saying their
lives don't matter.

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:55.960
So when you think about
gangbangers or things

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:58.730
in movies, we have to be
careful how we tell the story.

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:00.910
But if you give
them life and reason

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:03.670
and make them the point
of view in which we

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:07.360
are telling the story, then
that's a different thing.

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:10.750
If you're using them
frivolously in a movie

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:13.720
as a caricature of what
people think they should be,

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:14.970
then that's offensive.

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.630
But in storytelling,
I think it's

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:19.170
important to make sure that
we do tell those stories.

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:23.020
If you saw Demián
Bichir in A Better Life,

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:29.830
your heart ripped out of your
body because he was a father

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:31.300
trying to protect his son.

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:35.440
And if we humanize the
people of our community

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:38.830
and we give them stories and
lives that are worth telling,

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:44.260
then people will maybe see
them as human instead of other

00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:46.810
and instead of from there
and instead of go back

00:35:46.810 --> 00:35:48.460
to where you came from.

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:52.510
I thought, once this children
in cages on the border happened,

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:54.580
I thought, oh, moms
everywhere, we're

00:35:54.580 --> 00:35:58.180
going to get so much support
because any mother knows

00:35:58.180 --> 00:35:58.930
how that feels.

00:35:58.930 --> 00:35:59.812
And we didn't.

00:35:59.812 --> 00:36:00.520
And you know why?

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:02.920
Because we haven't
shown that these people

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:06.460
at the border crossing with
their children are moms.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:07.870
We have to humanize
these people.

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:10.150
And so sometimes when you
talk about stereotypes,

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:12.767
when I think about it in the
narrative storytelling way,

00:36:12.767 --> 00:36:14.350
I think we have to
tell those stories.

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:17.380
We can't shy away
and not tell them.

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:19.190
That's such a great point, Eva.

00:36:19.190 --> 00:36:22.210
And I think it leads us
into the next question

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:27.040
about our invisibility, too, as
a community and the data that

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:32.260
has been compiled, particularly
by Stacy Smith at USC,

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:34.990
that in front of the
camera, behind the camera,

00:36:34.990 --> 00:36:36.040
we're just missing.

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:38.470
We're 18% of the
population in this country,

00:36:38.470 --> 00:36:45.042
and yet we are in the low
4%, 3% in terms of just

00:36:45.042 --> 00:36:46.000
in front of the camera.

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:48.820
So everybody, jump in, please.

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:52.510
Eva, I'm sure you have a
perspective on this as well.

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:55.510
I have very strong perspective
on this because I think--

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:57.820
and I'll be quick--
but no matter

00:36:57.820 --> 00:37:01.435
what part of the film
ecosystem we're talking about,

00:37:01.435 --> 00:37:03.310
Latinos are vastly
underrepresented-- writer,

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:06.490
director, producer,
actor, casting director,

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:11.140
stunt coordinator, crew members,
first ACEs, camera operator.

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:15.920
Every part of the pipeline
you do not see Latinos.

00:37:15.920 --> 00:37:18.130
And this is at a time where
Latinos in our country

00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:22.000
are facing intense
concern about our safety.

00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:25.940
We are being attacked
daily in our communities.

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:29.660
And so to have authentic
and accurate representation

00:37:29.660 --> 00:37:31.440
throughout entertainment
is important.

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:34.270
And the interesting thing
about Stacy Smith's study

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:36.580
is she didn't call it
underrepresentation of Latinos

00:37:36.580 --> 00:37:37.750
in Hollywood.

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:41.560
She called it the erasure of
Latino community, the erasure,

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:45.610
because what
happens is that void

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:50.110
that is happening in the
entertainment industry

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:55.840
is being filled by hateful,
violent rhetoric through news.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:59.790
And so it's imperative
that talented storytellers

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:01.540
from our community are
given opportunities

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:06.220
to tell diverse and vibrant
narratives because it actually

00:38:06.220 --> 00:38:11.020
boils down to our
safety in this country.

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:14.380
I'd also like to
make a point here

00:38:14.380 --> 00:38:18.880
as it relates to invisibility
because really where

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:23.100
it all begins is that we are
absent from our history books.

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:27.340
So K through 12,
there is absolutely--

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:29.140
we're not educating
our own children

00:38:29.140 --> 00:38:31.450
nor are we educating
other children

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:32.740
about our contribution.

00:38:32.740 --> 00:38:35.320
Then you go into higher
learning-- same thing.

00:38:35.320 --> 00:38:37.900
So we are completely erased.

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:42.145
And so Hollywood, that has such
an incredible opportunity to--

00:38:42.145 --> 00:38:44.540
it actually shapes perception--

00:38:44.540 --> 00:38:46.300
they're erasing us completely.

00:38:46.300 --> 00:38:50.530
So therefore, that's
why, to Eva's point,

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:52.720
there are babies,
brown babies in cages,

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:56.660
and people can walk and just
sleep at night with no problem.

00:38:56.660 --> 00:39:01.180
So it's a really large problem,
especially when you look at,

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.570
not only 18% of the population--
our purchasing power,

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:06.430
the fact that this
economy does not

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:08.310
work without American Latinos.

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:09.870
It just doesn't.

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:12.070
And also, if you look
at our population--

00:39:12.070 --> 00:39:15.070
first of all, globe population,
let's look at the numbers.

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:17.280
Like, what percentage
of people in the world

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:19.420
are white versus the rest of us?

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:21.947
Like, it's just
common sense numbers.

00:39:21.947 --> 00:39:22.530
It's business.

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:24.060
It's catching up with Hollywood.

00:39:24.060 --> 00:39:27.300
So at this point, it's like, you
got to start from the very top,

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:29.820
like the board of
directors, the CEOs,

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:31.710
because it's all about money.

00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:35.910
It's obviously
also about healing,

00:39:35.910 --> 00:39:37.530
to be able to see us as human.

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:40.290
There are 60 million plus
stories in this country

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:42.900
alone that you're
missing out on.

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:46.320
And you just keep perpetuating
the same story over and over

00:39:46.320 --> 00:39:48.010
again.

00:39:48.010 --> 00:39:51.180
And I think that, growing
up in this environment,

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:53.310
I learned to code switch
at a very early age.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:55.350
And I didn't even
truly understand it

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:59.430
until the last decade,
really understand that--

00:39:59.430 --> 00:40:01.168
I was proud that I
could sit in a room

00:40:01.168 --> 00:40:03.210
with a bunch of white
people and fit in because I

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:04.980
spoke English in a certain way.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:07.590
And it wasn't until I
realized how much that really

00:40:07.590 --> 00:40:08.550
has affected me.

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:10.590
I am Puerto Rican.

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:14.100
In my blood is African ancestry.

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:18.480
It is European ancestry
and Indigenous.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:21.810
And I am the colonized
and the colonizer.

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:26.872
And so I think that we're now
coming together as Latinos

00:40:26.872 --> 00:40:28.830
from all walks of life,
whether you're Mexican,

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:30.000
Puerto-- wherever it is.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:32.295
And I feel like we're
coming together more now

00:40:32.295 --> 00:40:34.920
and we're really truly not going
to ask for permission anymore.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:36.045
And we're working together.

00:40:36.045 --> 00:40:37.320
I'm seeing that.

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:40.580
I'm seeing a
movement in that way.

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:43.980
It's also that people
always think that Latinos

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:47.460
are the dark skinned people.

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:51.490
I am as Latina as they come,
but I never get involved.

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:53.228
I never get included.

00:40:53.228 --> 00:40:54.520
Well, you're white, aren't you?

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.090
No, I'm not.

00:40:56.090 --> 00:40:56.590
I'm not.

00:40:56.590 --> 00:40:58.132
Well, Argentinians
are kind of white.

00:40:58.132 --> 00:41:00.610
I'm like, [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:02.540
I mean, that's the
other side of the coin.

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.450
Yeah, we tan well,
as you can see.

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:07.240
I mean, I've been out of my
yard for a long time now.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:11.260
187 days, but, hey,
who's counting?

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:16.660
But I'm sure, Carmen, you
probably suffer some of that,

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:20.650
although you do have
American heritage.

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:23.800
But mine is Italian Spaniard.

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:27.220
And where do we fit in?

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:28.900
I mean, if I don't
say my last name,

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:31.490
people think I'm from Holland.

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:32.310
Don't ask me why.

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:37.870
But I've been asked if I'm
Dutch more times than Greek

00:41:37.870 --> 00:41:39.370
or Italian or Spanish.

00:41:39.370 --> 00:41:41.260
I mean, I don't know.

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:43.653
So even within our community--

00:41:43.653 --> 00:41:45.070
I'm not just saying
white America.

00:41:45.070 --> 00:41:48.220
I'm telling you
Latinos in America--

00:41:48.220 --> 00:41:53.680
we don't even know how vast our
palette of shades and colors

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:54.670
we are.

00:41:54.670 --> 00:41:58.450
And we tend to
exclude one another

00:41:58.450 --> 00:42:01.870
by doing that unless
somebody sees your last name

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:02.900
or hears an accent.

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:05.080
I mean, I tick every box.

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:07.720
I came here very young
as an exchange student.

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:08.380
I was 15.

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:10.210
And then at 19, I moved.

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:12.580
I didn't speak English
when I was little.

00:42:12.580 --> 00:42:15.400
So English is my
second language,

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:18.690
always has been
and always will be.

00:42:18.690 --> 00:42:20.680
A female-- you can't deny.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:22.810
It's what it is, although
I've been treated as one

00:42:22.810 --> 00:42:24.430
of the guys my entire life.

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:27.040
I was always the only
female in the room

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:31.840
until I was able to be a part
of a group of people that

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:34.090
make the change so
that you no longer are,

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:36.220
because I also don't
understand that part of it.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:40.300
There's a lot of women that
love to be the only ones.

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:46.370
And I'd love to have those women
come and chat with us, please.

00:42:46.370 --> 00:42:48.800
Florencita, invite them.

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:49.820
OK, we'll find them.

00:42:49.820 --> 00:42:51.130
They're easy to find.

00:42:51.130 --> 00:42:54.000
That is not the
kind of shining star

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:57.960
that you should have
as a badge of honor.

00:42:57.960 --> 00:42:59.350
And I'm gay.

00:42:59.350 --> 00:43:01.600
So that [SOUND]
take another one.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:05.030
And I have an adopted child.

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:06.670
[SOUND], I mean, you name it.

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:07.690
Bring it on.

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:08.500
I'm over 50.

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:11.130
So I'm a senior.

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:13.480
Don't you mess with me, girls.

00:43:13.480 --> 00:43:14.110
I'm a senior.

00:43:14.110 --> 00:43:16.840
So more respect.

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:18.970
So I mean, it's one
after the other.

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:22.150
But within our community, we
tend to discriminate our own

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:23.480
as well.

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:24.790
You know what I mean?

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:26.410
I think that may
have to do a little

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:31.960
with our post-colonial
legacy, too, of class and race

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:38.220
and all of the baggage that we
carry from the colonization.

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:41.580
I think the class conversation
is a big one with regard to,

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:43.260
why are we not
seen in Hollywood,

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:48.600
because who is making,
even who is making content

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:56.430
and who is deciding what's
important versus what's

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:59.940
whatever the opposite
of important is.

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:02.250
Their films have to
have been important.

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:07.740
And that's very interesting
when the group that

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:13.870
is making that decision
is primarily not

00:44:13.870 --> 00:44:18.390
of color or not
upper middle class

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:21.030
or not all of those things.

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:24.400
I think it's very important
to consider all of that.

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:26.835
And I think, fortunately,
that's been happening.

00:44:26.835 --> 00:44:27.960
It's been being considered.

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:30.502
The Academy letting all these
people in in the last few years

00:44:30.502 --> 00:44:33.210
has been a great
step toward that.

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:35.340
But still, there's language.

00:44:35.340 --> 00:44:38.550
I always think about
that, the important.

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:42.480
How are we deciding
who's deciding?

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:46.020
I want to make sure that
I pick on your phrase

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:47.715
the Academy letting people.

00:44:47.715 --> 00:44:49.590
Oh my god, I was going
to say the same thing.

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:53.750
The Academy is inviting
people that deserve to be in.

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:55.600
They're not letting them in.

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:57.080
To say that we're
letting them in

00:44:57.080 --> 00:44:59.950
is saying that they have
no place on earth being

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:00.450
[INAUDIBLE].

00:45:00.450 --> 00:45:01.870
It goes with my feisty.

00:45:01.870 --> 00:45:02.670
You invited me.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.650
Here I am.

00:45:04.650 --> 00:45:07.050
We belong.

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:09.930
We have a place in your table.

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:12.810
The Academy is now
taking a look of who

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:15.640
are the guests for
that dinner table.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.360
And we are invited to join.

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:21.240
In fact, they can decline
the invite, by the way.

00:45:21.240 --> 00:45:24.090
They don't have to say yes.

00:45:24.090 --> 00:45:27.120
But I think that is
so important in part

00:45:27.120 --> 00:45:31.130
of that is, not only the
patriarchy but in just this

00:45:31.130 --> 00:45:36.150
and how we attack this
inclusivity and equality,

00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:39.550
that we change how we
talk about our people

00:45:39.550 --> 00:45:41.370
allowing us to come in.

00:45:41.370 --> 00:45:42.570
You don't get to allow me.

00:45:46.265 --> 00:45:47.140
And that's the thing.

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:47.890
It's not charity.

00:45:47.890 --> 00:45:48.970
It's equality.

00:45:48.970 --> 00:45:50.710
And there are many
qualified people

00:45:50.710 --> 00:45:52.710
over the years who
are not invited.

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:55.760
And so I was just going
to say the same thing.

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:58.390
It's not like, oh, thank
you so much for letting me

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:00.070
in because I did
not feel that way.

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:06.205
I felt like, we have so many
people of talented caliber

00:46:06.205 --> 00:46:08.080
in our community that
just aren't recognized.

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:11.890
The same thing with the
drama with American Dirt when

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:14.290
the author of that
Oprah Book Club said,

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:16.690
I wish somebody browner
than me wrote the book

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:17.440
about immigration.

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:19.150
And everybody in the
publishing industry,

00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:22.240
I mean, every author
goes, it has been written.

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:24.140
Oprah didn't pick them.

00:46:24.140 --> 00:46:28.020
Enrique so and so and Raquel--

00:46:28.020 --> 00:46:32.470
like, this book has been written
18 times by a person of color.

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:34.690
That book was not picked
by the publishing industry.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:35.680
Your book was picked.

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:41.410
And so that's what happens when
you don't have a Victoria that

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:43.990
has a seat at the table, when
you don't have a Lorenza who

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:49.600
has a seat at the table in
these very huge institutions

00:46:49.600 --> 00:46:52.000
that we feel sometimes
are impenetrable.

00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:54.007
So it's so important
that you guys are there.

00:46:54.007 --> 00:46:54.840
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:57.200
And, Carmen, I wasn't
picking on you.

00:46:57.200 --> 00:46:57.700
No.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:59.120
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:46:59.120 --> 00:46:59.660
Not at all.

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:04.030
[INAUDIBLE] sure that you
know this is not me and you.

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:06.580
The words matter, every word.

00:47:06.580 --> 00:47:07.850
Absolutely.

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:09.070
I completely get that.

00:47:09.070 --> 00:47:13.210
And actually, I appreciate
that you pointed that out

00:47:13.210 --> 00:47:15.310
because it also speaks
to maybe how did

00:47:15.310 --> 00:47:19.000
I feel when I got invited.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:22.480
It's a way to explore
your own connection to it.

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:26.810
But also, for me,
words do matter.

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:31.570
And for me, when we
talk about inclusion,

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:35.212
I always change it to
correcting exclusion.

00:47:35.212 --> 00:47:37.430
I love that.

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:39.440
Because I don't
want to be included.

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:42.270
I want you to fix
that you left me out.

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:42.770
I love that.

00:47:42.770 --> 00:47:43.603
I'm going to use it.

00:47:43.603 --> 00:47:44.390
Can I use it?

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:45.180
Absolutely.

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:45.680
I love it.

00:47:45.680 --> 00:47:46.940
I mean, but that's real.

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:48.600
That's the thing about language.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:52.650
Language is so important with
all of this, especially for us.

00:47:52.650 --> 00:47:57.980
Yeah, language, perspective,
and you all have--

00:47:57.980 --> 00:48:00.750
Carmen certainly has been
fighting the good fight.

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:04.850
Victoria, I don't need to tell
you what a fighter that one is.

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:08.410
And thank god, she's
been in the room with us

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:10.370
and Ivette and Nadia as well.

00:48:10.370 --> 00:48:13.790
I mean, it's been a
very interesting thing

00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:15.470
to observe at the
Academy, how we

00:48:15.470 --> 00:48:21.890
broaden people's perspectives,
shall we say, in a nice way.

00:48:21.890 --> 00:48:23.750
I wanted to just
make a point, also.

00:48:23.750 --> 00:48:29.600
It's the people in power,
they have this European POV.

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:31.370
And they're surrounded
by people who

00:48:31.370 --> 00:48:34.520
look like them, talk like
them, think like them,

00:48:34.520 --> 00:48:36.170
and have the same background.

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:39.350
And so that's where
we really need

00:48:39.350 --> 00:48:44.210
to see change happen, because
we can keep talking about this.

00:48:44.210 --> 00:48:46.920
And I've been in those
rooms as well, Carmen.

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:48.080
Yeah, I see that.

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:50.330
And it's sort of shocking.

00:48:50.330 --> 00:48:54.650
Our perspectives
and our stories are

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:58.490
in a chokehold in the Hollywood
ecosystem and structure.

00:48:58.490 --> 00:49:02.660
And so until we
are in that room,

00:49:02.660 --> 00:49:04.070
I'm afraid we're
not going to see

00:49:04.070 --> 00:49:08.030
the change that we need to see.

00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:09.550
Nadia, you wanted
to say something,

00:49:09.550 --> 00:49:10.717
and we kept cutting you off.

00:49:10.717 --> 00:49:11.290
I'm so sorry.

00:49:11.290 --> 00:49:12.492
No, it's interesting.

00:49:12.492 --> 00:49:14.200
I had the an interesting
experience, even

00:49:14.200 --> 00:49:19.390
when you mentioned the
backlash about your film.

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:21.940
I made a documentary
called After Maria.

00:49:21.940 --> 00:49:25.720
And for my whole
life, I wanted to make

00:49:25.720 --> 00:49:29.920
something that could explore
Puerto Rican identity in the US

00:49:29.920 --> 00:49:32.740
and just the
complexity of what that

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:39.880
is to be a citizen of the United
States, not speak the language,

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:42.820
be called all types of names and
all these things and something

00:49:42.820 --> 00:49:44.080
that I always wanted to do.

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:47.780
So when Hurricane
Maria happened,

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:49.590
I decided to make a film.

00:49:49.590 --> 00:49:52.850
I knew I wanted to make a film
around the story of families

00:49:52.850 --> 00:49:55.430
that were leaving the island
and coming to New York

00:49:55.430 --> 00:50:00.560
to find a new life, not die on
the island, all the things that

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:01.370
were happening.

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:04.238
And so I ended up
telling the story.

00:50:04.238 --> 00:50:05.780
I made a documentary
about three moms

00:50:05.780 --> 00:50:10.610
who met on the same floor in a
FEMA hotel and the relationship

00:50:10.610 --> 00:50:14.060
that they built
between them, trying

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:18.110
to survive the bureaucracy
of FEMA and the experience

00:50:18.110 --> 00:50:20.150
that they were having.

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:24.260
So the film gets
greenlit by Netflix

00:50:24.260 --> 00:50:26.210
and they take this movie.

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:31.400
And so, as they started to
play the trailer of the film,

00:50:31.400 --> 00:50:34.850
there was all this
excitement around this film,

00:50:34.850 --> 00:50:36.530
because we made it
relatively quickly.

00:50:36.530 --> 00:50:39.770
And Puerto Rico was
still in the news,

00:50:39.770 --> 00:50:43.040
and people were just like, I
can't wait to see this movie.

00:50:43.040 --> 00:50:46.400
This is going to be like the
tell all film of what happened

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:48.410
in Puerto Rico, the exposé.

00:50:48.410 --> 00:50:49.340
And it wasn't.

00:50:49.340 --> 00:50:51.680
It was a character story
about three moms that

00:50:51.680 --> 00:50:54.710
became friends and helped
raise each other's kids

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:56.210
and survive this experience.

00:50:56.210 --> 00:51:00.440
But we had such a
tremendous backlash

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:05.540
on this film from the Puerto
Rican community mainly--

00:51:05.540 --> 00:51:06.530
for a couple reasons.

00:51:06.530 --> 00:51:12.660
One was people had these high
hopes of seeing themselves,

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:16.320
every version of
themselves, in this movie.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:20.520
So people felt it should have
been this like epic eight part

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:23.050
Netflix series about what
happened in Hurricane Maria,

00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:24.930
and it was not.

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:27.090
And so anyone that didn't
feel like their story was

00:51:27.090 --> 00:51:30.460
represented was angry.

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:34.590
And then the film
was made by women

00:51:34.590 --> 00:51:38.880
who I fell in love with that
reminded me of my own family

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:40.530
members, my own mother.

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:43.170
And there were
issues around class.

00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:47.280
And some people felt that I
had portrayed low class Puerto

00:51:47.280 --> 00:51:49.050
Ricans in this film.

00:51:49.050 --> 00:51:53.310
And it was such an
interesting experiment

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:57.000
for me to have this
experience where

00:51:57.000 --> 00:51:59.880
I made something
that is so dignified

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:01.060
and heartfelt and beautiful.

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:04.540
And people had all these
different interpretations

00:52:04.540 --> 00:52:05.040
of it.

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:09.300
And then at the same time, I
had so many people that said,

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:10.800
I can't believe you
told this story.

00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:12.090
This is so amazing.

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:14.490
I see myself in
this, and I've never

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:17.680
seen Puerto Ricans on screen
with this level of complexity.

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:21.810
And so what it showed
me was that there's

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:24.480
a huge void in
Hollywood and what

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:27.330
we're seeing on television,
where everyone holds

00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:32.370
onto this idea that this
one film, which was like 38

00:52:32.370 --> 00:52:35.100
minutes, was going
to tell everyone's

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:39.210
story and the disappointment
that existed when they didn't

00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:43.170
feel like their story was
told and how much more we need

00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:45.390
to tell stories so that
people can see themselves.

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:48.360
But since all of our
identities are so different,

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:50.670
that's not going to
happen with one film

00:52:50.670 --> 00:52:54.990
and for platforms and
streamers and whomever

00:52:54.990 --> 00:52:57.930
to understand that there's
also this desperate desire

00:52:57.930 --> 00:53:00.160
to see this from an audience.

00:53:00.160 --> 00:53:03.270
And there's so much
opportunity that's

00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:05.800
not being given to filmmakers.

00:53:05.800 --> 00:53:08.570
Well, you bring up such
an important point,

00:53:08.570 --> 00:53:11.380
because if you're
the only movie,

00:53:11.380 --> 00:53:13.750
they're going to tear it
apart for all the reasons.

00:53:13.750 --> 00:53:16.540
When you're one of
the many movies,

00:53:16.540 --> 00:53:20.050
then you can always reference,
well, it portrayed this well

00:53:20.050 --> 00:53:22.310
but it didn't do it like
x movie or that movie.

00:53:22.310 --> 00:53:24.670
But you're the only
movie to do it.

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:27.460
So you have nothing
but to do it right.

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:29.170
And it's impossible
to do it right.

00:53:29.170 --> 00:53:34.030
That's why one of the things
that Eva said when she started

00:53:34.030 --> 00:53:36.880
is like, I don't
want to do this one.

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:40.030
When I step on set, I need
to check this, this, this.

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:41.890
It's like, no, you
have to direct.

00:53:41.890 --> 00:53:43.750
As long as you get
your story right,

00:53:43.750 --> 00:53:47.350
who cares if you're wearing a
jean shirt or a regular t-shirt

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:50.735
or a low cut or no cut?

00:53:50.735 --> 00:53:51.610
You know what I mean?

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:56.350
That kind of pressure,
it puts the spotlight on,

00:53:56.350 --> 00:54:01.640
there is true racism
within the Latin community.

00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:02.530
You know what I mean?

00:54:02.530 --> 00:54:03.988
The higher class
doesn't want to be

00:54:03.988 --> 00:54:05.210
portrayed as the lower class.

00:54:05.210 --> 00:54:06.760
The lower class
doesn't always want

00:54:06.760 --> 00:54:08.500
to be portrayed
as the people that

00:54:08.500 --> 00:54:11.900
need things because a lot of
them are trying not to need it.

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:13.990
And I mean, it's constant.

00:54:16.870 --> 00:54:20.920
And then my identity
as a Puerto Rican

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:23.610
was totally put into question.

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:25.270
I'm an Nuyorican from the Bronx.

00:54:25.270 --> 00:54:28.060
I don't speak Spanish.

00:54:28.060 --> 00:54:29.302
I'm Afro Puerto Rican.

00:54:29.302 --> 00:54:30.760
I'm not light
skinned Puerto Rican.

00:54:30.760 --> 00:54:32.670
What do you know?

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:34.730
I'm like, this is insane.

00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:37.540
But it also was so cathartic
at the same time that--

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:46.330
I think once you get over
the initial processing,

00:54:46.330 --> 00:54:49.060
I felt like I was really able
to explore something deep

00:54:49.060 --> 00:54:51.520
within myself through
my work that I had been

00:54:51.520 --> 00:54:53.540
wanting to do my whole life.

00:54:53.540 --> 00:54:57.460
Yeah, I think also what
Stacy's report did also

00:54:57.460 --> 00:55:01.550
was offer solutions
because I think sometimes

00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:04.410
big studios are paralyzed
by this problem.

00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:07.780
And they think, just
cast, cast, cast, cast.

00:55:07.780 --> 00:55:09.270
And you're like, no, no, no.

00:55:09.270 --> 00:55:12.850
We need change across casting,
building a pipeline for Latino

00:55:12.850 --> 00:55:15.760
filmmakers, whether it's writers
or producers or directors,

00:55:15.760 --> 00:55:18.580
urging studios to
hire Latino talent

00:55:18.580 --> 00:55:20.920
for advertising
and the trailers.

00:55:20.920 --> 00:55:24.260
And there's companies that
do that are Latino owned.

00:55:24.260 --> 00:55:29.800
So making sure we're inclusive
in the entire ecosystem

00:55:29.800 --> 00:55:32.890
of the industry
and in the industry

00:55:32.890 --> 00:55:36.760
sectors would really help--

00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:37.960
it would go a long way.

00:55:37.960 --> 00:55:40.830
And I think sometimes we
only focus on casting--

00:55:40.830 --> 00:55:42.070
like cast more, cast more.

00:55:42.070 --> 00:55:44.560
You're like, no, no, no
We need so much more

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:47.110
in the infrastructure of
opportunity that has to be

00:55:47.110 --> 00:55:48.400
created for us.

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:52.420
But it does go to
the some Latin kids,

00:55:52.420 --> 00:55:55.750
young adults don't
know these doors

00:55:55.750 --> 00:56:00.670
exist except for on camera,
because that's all they see.

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:02.500
They don't see what I do.

00:56:02.500 --> 00:56:05.530
They don't see what Ivette does
or what Carmen or Nadia does.

00:56:05.530 --> 00:56:09.680
They only see, Eva, what you
do or what you used to do.

00:56:09.680 --> 00:56:11.950
I know you're directing,
and you've always produced.

00:56:11.950 --> 00:56:19.310
But we need to inform
and educate our own.

00:56:19.310 --> 00:56:22.070
I know, Ivette, you and I
and Lorenza, of course, we

00:56:22.070 --> 00:56:23.760
do a lot of things together.

00:56:23.760 --> 00:56:27.680
But this is why I encourage
everyone to do the panels,

00:56:27.680 --> 00:56:31.460
go to the schools, go to
conferences, show your faces,

00:56:31.460 --> 00:56:33.380
tell them, it happened to me.

00:56:33.380 --> 00:56:35.310
I didn't know anybody.

00:56:35.310 --> 00:56:36.460
I didn't know anybody.

00:56:36.460 --> 00:56:40.370
And I came from far away so
that kids understand, oh, I

00:56:40.370 --> 00:56:41.030
could do that.

00:56:41.030 --> 00:56:44.270
Oh, I don't have to have
a 17 year degree to do it,

00:56:44.270 --> 00:56:46.820
because I just have a
high school diploma.

00:56:46.820 --> 00:56:47.420
It's OK.

00:56:47.420 --> 00:56:48.320
I'll teach you.

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:49.058
It's all good.

00:56:49.058 --> 00:56:49.850
You don't have to--

00:56:49.850 --> 00:56:52.100
I mean, not to say I don't
want them to go to college.

00:56:52.100 --> 00:56:57.860
But it's important for us to
teach the young generations

00:56:57.860 --> 00:57:01.970
to know that you don't have to
have all the things that they

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:04.760
think they have to in
order to be a part of it

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:07.220
if you don't do it
in front of camera.

00:57:07.220 --> 00:57:11.835
And on that note, I
know Eva has to run.

00:57:11.835 --> 00:57:12.578
I have to go.

00:57:12.578 --> 00:57:14.120
But I want to say,
I love you ladies.

00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:15.267
I love you so much.

00:57:15.267 --> 00:57:16.100
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:57:16.100 --> 00:57:18.180
It so great talking to you.

00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:20.270
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:22.280
And now I will turn
it over to Shawn Finnie

00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:24.670
for our final questions.

00:57:24.670 --> 00:57:26.600
That was so amazing,
so beautiful.

00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:29.150
Thank you all so much for coming
and just sharing your light

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:30.840
in your stories.

00:57:30.840 --> 00:57:34.010
Ivette, I wanted to start with
you just because you mentioned

00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:36.830
something about what I think
is so important in the Latino

00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:39.230
community, is that you
are so many things.

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:42.800
So a lot of everyone can
be ethnically ambiguous.

00:57:42.800 --> 00:57:44.450
Do you often-- and
for all of you--

00:57:44.450 --> 00:57:47.870
do you feel misunderstood
because they may not

00:57:47.870 --> 00:57:50.060
understand, Victoria,
you're from Argentina?

00:57:50.060 --> 00:57:51.730
And Nadia, you're a Nuyorican.

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:52.730
And Ivette, you're this.

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:55.430
Do often feel
completely misunderstood

00:57:55.430 --> 00:57:59.660
in the community as well as
people not in the community?

00:57:59.660 --> 00:58:02.050
In the Latino community
or in the industry?

00:58:02.050 --> 00:58:05.000
In the industry.

00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:09.980
Yeah, they are perplexed.

00:58:09.980 --> 00:58:12.910
They don't know quite what I am.

00:58:12.910 --> 00:58:14.590
I think that there is a lot--

00:58:14.590 --> 00:58:16.520
I mean I spoke about it
a little bit earlier.

00:58:16.520 --> 00:58:20.590
But our educational
system, we are completely

00:58:20.590 --> 00:58:22.840
erased from our history
and our contribution.

00:58:22.840 --> 00:58:24.560
So nobody understands
the difference.

00:58:24.560 --> 00:58:27.700
And I didn't even
know my own history

00:58:27.700 --> 00:58:31.000
until I went out and bought a
bunch of books on Puerto Rico.

00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:32.650
And then that's when I learned--

00:58:32.650 --> 00:58:34.810
and then I got my DNA,
and I could see the map.

00:58:34.810 --> 00:58:37.270
I'm like, oh, West Africa,
North Africa, here I go.

00:58:37.270 --> 00:58:39.370
I mean, it was just
fascinating to me.

00:58:39.370 --> 00:58:41.440
That's not something I
learned in a history book

00:58:41.440 --> 00:58:42.775
or in advanced learning.

00:58:42.775 --> 00:58:44.150
And [INAUDIBLE]
so we don't have.

00:58:44.150 --> 00:58:46.510
And I'm really excited
about, fingers crossed,

00:58:46.510 --> 00:58:48.518
the American Latino
museum on the Hill.

00:58:48.518 --> 00:58:50.560
That's going to be a
beautiful place where people

00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:54.280
can come and learn about
our culture and our history

00:58:54.280 --> 00:58:56.860
and in Hollywood.

00:58:56.860 --> 00:58:58.570
Hollywood could help
us get there quicker

00:58:58.570 --> 00:59:00.670
because everything else
is going to take a minute.

00:59:00.670 --> 00:59:03.160
But Hollywood has
the power right now,

00:59:03.160 --> 00:59:05.980
right here to make a
difference and to help

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:08.380
us tell our stories so that
people aren't afraid of us

00:59:08.380 --> 00:59:10.240
and they're not stereotyping us.

00:59:10.240 --> 00:59:12.970
So really, it's Hollywood.

00:59:12.970 --> 00:59:15.040
I'm talking to Hollywood.

00:59:15.040 --> 00:59:17.780
This is not rocket science.

00:59:17.780 --> 00:59:19.970
It's really not.

00:59:19.970 --> 00:59:23.743
I think it's important not to
dilute your story to blend in.

00:59:23.743 --> 00:59:25.410
And I think what we've
been trying to do

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:28.030
for so long is to blend in.

00:59:28.030 --> 00:59:28.530
Why?

00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:30.540
Because it's better that way.

00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:33.820
If you blend in, they're
not going to single you out.

00:59:33.820 --> 00:59:38.190
And you can't not
blend in this female.

00:59:38.190 --> 00:59:39.960
If that's how you
define yourself,

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:44.957
then you have to just
blend in every other way.

00:59:44.957 --> 00:59:46.290
And you're going to have enough.

00:59:46.290 --> 00:59:48.165
You're going to be
discriminated as a female.

00:59:48.165 --> 00:59:49.530
So just shut up.

00:59:49.530 --> 00:59:50.120
Don't sit.

00:59:50.120 --> 00:59:53.100
Just, if you have an
accent, work on it so people

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:54.690
don't hear it.

00:59:54.690 --> 00:59:56.360
I refuse to work on my accent.

00:59:56.360 --> 00:59:57.870
It is what it is.

00:59:57.870 --> 01:00:00.823
Either you like it or you
don't like it, I love it.

01:00:00.823 --> 01:00:02.490
And don't get me
pissed off because I'll

01:00:02.490 --> 01:00:04.860
start rattling in Spanish,
and I'll just say it all.

01:00:04.860 --> 01:00:06.510
And that's just how it goes.

01:00:06.510 --> 01:00:08.630
Lorenza, [SPEAKING SPANISH].

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:09.720
It's just how it goes.

01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:13.287
I mean, so many
times people say--

01:00:13.287 --> 01:00:15.120
I love it when you start
singing in Spanish.

01:00:15.120 --> 01:00:15.620
I do.

01:00:18.510 --> 01:00:21.060
People used to say to
me, are you Italian,

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:25.920
and that invite, that sexy
invite to say, yes, yes, yes,

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:28.080
so that we can actually--

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:29.790
I'm like, well, my
grandparents were.

01:00:29.790 --> 01:00:33.000
But, no, I am full
on Argentinian.

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:35.100
And that was [SOUND].

01:00:35.100 --> 01:00:37.230
And Victoria, do
you remember when

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:39.520
it was the Italians
and the Irish who

01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:40.645
were discriminated against?

01:00:40.645 --> 01:00:41.978
So that's the interesting thing.

01:00:41.978 --> 01:00:42.810
Now they're cool.

01:00:42.810 --> 01:00:43.694
Totally.

01:00:43.694 --> 01:00:44.580
Totally.

01:00:44.580 --> 01:00:45.860
Everything has a flow.

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:51.300
But I think that the more of
us that are out there talking

01:00:51.300 --> 01:00:55.530
about it, the more of our
stories, the more times

01:00:55.530 --> 01:00:59.430
that Nadia gets to tell
that story that Puerto Rican

01:00:59.430 --> 01:01:03.810
or Nuyorican, however you
want to call it, Nadia--

01:01:03.810 --> 01:01:08.250
and maybe in your next film,
you tell the other story

01:01:08.250 --> 01:01:10.980
After Maria because
those were three mothers.

01:01:10.980 --> 01:01:15.867
There were thousands--
thousands-- that were affected.

01:01:15.867 --> 01:01:17.700
And maybe is there one
after the earthquake.

01:01:17.700 --> 01:01:18.510
Who knows?

01:01:18.510 --> 01:01:21.540
But you told a different
story would be coming.

01:01:21.540 --> 01:01:26.580
And it's always enriching the
fact that, behind that camera,

01:01:26.580 --> 01:01:29.485
there is a person
that represents us.

01:01:29.485 --> 01:01:30.360
You know what I mean?

01:01:30.360 --> 01:01:32.280
And then we can talk
about it, and the minute

01:01:32.280 --> 01:01:34.980
that people see it,
that it happened to you,

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:39.070
and it happened to me, and
Lorenza and Carmen and Ivette

01:01:39.070 --> 01:01:40.810
and Eva and many others--

01:01:40.810 --> 01:01:45.150
you don't have to be Penelope
Cruz or Salma Hayek or Eva

01:01:45.150 --> 01:01:46.110
Longoria.

01:01:46.110 --> 01:01:48.510
There's other women out
there that are doing it.

01:01:48.510 --> 01:01:50.010
And that's the most
important thing.

01:01:50.010 --> 01:01:55.260
You can be in any other
place in the film industry.

01:01:55.260 --> 01:01:57.060
And there's so many
places that you

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:59.650
can be to make a difference.

01:01:59.650 --> 01:02:00.230
Absolutely.

01:02:00.230 --> 01:02:03.940
And I do think--
to speak, Victoria,

01:02:03.940 --> 01:02:04.940
to what you were saying.

01:02:04.940 --> 01:02:07.940
Specificity-- for me it's been
very exciting in these last,

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:11.780
I think, only really
five years to see

01:02:11.780 --> 01:02:16.310
these voices telling their
own stories, our own stories.

01:02:16.310 --> 01:02:20.870
And I think until there is real
specificity to the narratives,

01:02:20.870 --> 01:02:23.780
it is going to be like,
oh, here's a cast.

01:02:23.780 --> 01:02:26.830
Make the supporting person
Latin or make her Black.

01:02:26.830 --> 01:02:29.760
And even for me,
who is all about,

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:32.060
as I said, correcting
exclusion, I

01:02:32.060 --> 01:02:34.940
don't find that to be
any kind of solution.

01:02:34.940 --> 01:02:38.990
I actually find that
to be somewhat--

01:02:38.990 --> 01:02:40.190
I don't want to say racist.

01:02:40.190 --> 01:02:43.160
But it doesn't
solve any problems.

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:45.500
But it makes people feel
like something has happened.

01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:47.150
And I don't like
participating in that.

01:02:47.150 --> 01:02:51.350
If there's a story reason
for the supporting character

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:54.650
to be Latin or Black,
then fantastic.

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:58.040
Or if you're going to
change that story, if you're

01:02:58.040 --> 01:03:00.890
going to enrich that character
in a way that is specific, then

01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:01.850
great.

01:03:01.850 --> 01:03:04.460
But when you get these
storytellers telling

01:03:04.460 --> 01:03:05.960
their own stories,
that doesn't have

01:03:05.960 --> 01:03:07.980
to happen because it's natural.

01:03:07.980 --> 01:03:10.730
It's natural to the
story, just like it

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:13.010
is natural in our actual lives.

01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:14.660
Are you saying that
you'd rather not

01:03:14.660 --> 01:03:18.080
have those supporting
roles be of people of color

01:03:18.080 --> 01:03:20.240
because it's not
necessarily that story

01:03:20.240 --> 01:03:21.920
that they're telling?

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:25.610
I prefer if that-- and this
is something that I generally

01:03:25.610 --> 01:03:28.330
try to do, and I'm pretty
successful with it--

01:03:28.330 --> 01:03:31.400
in having a dialogue
with the filmmakers

01:03:31.400 --> 01:03:35.870
and saying like, great,
yes, but is there something

01:03:35.870 --> 01:03:38.930
that makes this relevant.

01:03:38.930 --> 01:03:41.570
I also agree with
trying to just make

01:03:41.570 --> 01:03:42.920
the entire thing more diverse.

01:03:42.920 --> 01:03:44.378
And it doesn't have
to be specific.

01:03:44.378 --> 01:03:47.210
But I'm saying for
us to move forward,

01:03:47.210 --> 01:03:49.490
even just a little tweak
of, give that person

01:03:49.490 --> 01:03:57.770
a reason to have been Latin
at the JPL or whatever, again,

01:03:57.770 --> 01:04:00.860
because I think it does
speak to the specific--

01:04:00.860 --> 01:04:02.750
I agree with you, Carmen.

01:04:02.750 --> 01:04:04.040
And actually, we both--

01:04:04.040 --> 01:04:05.750
I worked on Vida
on second season.

01:04:05.750 --> 01:04:07.730
I mean, you did an
amazing job with Vida.

01:04:07.730 --> 01:04:11.540
But for me as a marketer,
talking to our media

01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:16.670
and having to really talk to
all the organizations and say,

01:04:16.670 --> 01:04:18.860
I do not want you to
come out and start

01:04:18.860 --> 01:04:21.830
talking about the fact that
this is a story about LGBTQ

01:04:21.830 --> 01:04:23.600
and that's East Los An--

01:04:23.600 --> 01:04:26.030
I literally had to
speak to them in advance

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:29.000
to say, do not come out and
just-- this one story is

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:32.720
a very specific story that
was so beautifully told,

01:04:32.720 --> 01:04:35.120
that it's not everybody's story.

01:04:35.120 --> 01:04:39.800
And so specifically what you
did with that specific show,

01:04:39.800 --> 01:04:40.610
I mean, I loved it.

01:04:40.610 --> 01:04:43.190
I love every character
in that series.

01:04:43.190 --> 01:04:44.630
They're amazing.

01:04:44.630 --> 01:04:45.230
Thank you.

01:04:45.230 --> 01:04:45.320
It's a stories about sisters.

01:04:45.320 --> 01:04:45.903
I think it is.

01:04:45.903 --> 01:04:47.900
I think it's about
allowing ourselves

01:04:47.900 --> 01:04:49.700
to be our full selves.

01:04:49.700 --> 01:04:50.810
And in that one it is.

01:04:50.810 --> 01:04:52.160
It's about being sisters.

01:04:52.160 --> 01:04:53.990
That story is about
being sisters.

01:04:53.990 --> 01:04:54.590
That's it.

01:04:54.590 --> 01:04:55.620
It's universal.

01:04:55.620 --> 01:04:56.670
It's so universal.

01:04:56.670 --> 01:04:59.420
And the fact [INAUDIBLE] and one
is gay and the other one is not

01:04:59.420 --> 01:05:01.310
and then the mother--

01:05:01.310 --> 01:05:02.180
we're sisters.

01:05:02.180 --> 01:05:05.210
And I think that people
love to pigeonhole us

01:05:05.210 --> 01:05:06.500
into the one thing.

01:05:06.500 --> 01:05:08.330
And I understand
they have to sell it.

01:05:08.330 --> 01:05:12.440
And I understand they have
to, in this moment in time,

01:05:12.440 --> 01:05:18.250
they have to look good and
say, no, no, we are inclusive

01:05:18.250 --> 01:05:22.010
or we're trying to correct
exclusion, as Carmen says.

01:05:22.010 --> 01:05:25.100
That's my favorite line of
it all, the whole day today.

01:05:25.100 --> 01:05:26.900
I'm so glad that I heard it.

01:05:26.900 --> 01:05:31.680
But I think it's important
that, can we just be women.

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:36.070
Can we just be moms,
sisters, partners, friends?

01:05:36.070 --> 01:05:37.980
And then the moment
that we start

01:05:37.980 --> 01:05:41.040
taking the things that
makes us so different,

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:43.360
we start belonging.

01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:48.390
And we tell that to ourselves
and we tell that to society.

01:05:48.390 --> 01:05:52.380
If you look at every single
Netflix show that is here now

01:05:52.380 --> 01:05:53.400
from every country--

01:05:53.400 --> 01:05:55.380
I've seen them all
because I see everything

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:56.630
from all over the world--

01:05:56.630 --> 01:05:57.672
we're not that different.

01:05:57.672 --> 01:05:59.410
We all have the same problems.

01:05:59.410 --> 01:06:00.850
Absolutely.

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:01.350
Of course.

01:06:01.350 --> 01:06:03.510
I mean, we speak
another language.

01:06:03.510 --> 01:06:04.890
We may look a little different.

01:06:04.890 --> 01:06:07.140
But truly, it's about
the pursuit of happiness,

01:06:07.140 --> 01:06:11.640
the lack of friendship, the need
to decide, the fear of dying.

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:13.480
I mean, I can go on and on.

01:06:13.480 --> 01:06:15.560
It's universal.

01:06:15.560 --> 01:06:16.310
Absolutely.

01:06:16.310 --> 01:06:17.570
It's very much universal.

01:06:17.570 --> 01:06:20.660
And Netflix is such
a great platform.

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:21.830
I watch all of the same.

01:06:21.830 --> 01:06:22.820
I love it so much.

01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:25.120
I mean, I'm watching
every country,

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:27.770
and I always think to
myself, Hollywood studios,

01:06:27.770 --> 01:06:29.610
why, why can't you do this.

01:06:29.610 --> 01:06:32.180
Why can't you look at
what Netflix is doing?

01:06:32.180 --> 01:06:32.990
It's amazing.

01:06:32.990 --> 01:06:34.850
I mean, it's right
there for you just

01:06:34.850 --> 01:06:39.740
to understand that there is an
audience, a global audience.

01:06:39.740 --> 01:06:43.500
I think slowly but surely
everyone is getting the--

01:06:43.500 --> 01:06:45.140
they're hearing
the bell, which has

01:06:45.140 --> 01:06:47.462
been ringing for a long time.

01:06:47.462 --> 01:06:48.560
A long time.

01:06:48.560 --> 01:06:51.710
But you say something, Victoria,
that I know I'll never forget

01:06:51.710 --> 01:06:58.550
and that's, until
we're 30% of the room,

01:06:58.550 --> 01:07:02.465
we're really not
going to see a change.

01:07:02.465 --> 01:07:04.100
We don't turn the room.

01:07:04.100 --> 01:07:07.550
I always say that you can be
the one person or the 2% or 3%.

01:07:07.550 --> 01:07:11.870
The problem is 30%
has enough weight

01:07:11.870 --> 01:07:14.520
to start changing
the conversation.

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:17.690
You still can be toppled
by the other 70%.

01:07:17.690 --> 01:07:22.820
But when you're just one
voice in a room where

01:07:22.820 --> 01:07:25.550
there's 10 people, you're
just one single voice.

01:07:25.550 --> 01:07:26.540
And then you're two.

01:07:26.540 --> 01:07:29.690
Then it's just that pair
that is always [SOUNDS]..

01:07:29.690 --> 01:07:32.840
Then all of a sudden there's
three people talking about it.

01:07:32.840 --> 01:07:34.760
So it's not just the rattlers.

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:36.230
There's somebody else.

01:07:36.230 --> 01:07:40.160
And then somebody in
the room, again, of 10

01:07:40.160 --> 01:07:42.260
finds themselves
to have permission

01:07:42.260 --> 01:07:44.660
to hear what you're saying.

01:07:44.660 --> 01:07:48.620
They allow themselves to hear
it because more than one person

01:07:48.620 --> 01:07:49.580
says it.

01:07:49.580 --> 01:07:54.380
And then you turn to
start to be heard.

01:07:54.380 --> 01:07:55.940
You may be being listened to.

01:07:55.940 --> 01:07:57.410
But if you're not
heard, you're not

01:07:57.410 --> 01:08:00.110
going to turn the room
to where it needs to

01:08:00.110 --> 01:08:03.910
or, if nothing
else, to be heard.

01:08:03.910 --> 01:08:07.180
Sometimes we just want
you to consider it.

01:08:07.180 --> 01:08:12.310
Like Carmen is saying,
consider not excluding us.

01:08:12.310 --> 01:08:14.220
Just consider it.

01:08:14.220 --> 01:08:18.189
Consider giving that character
a backstory as opposed to Latin

01:08:18.189 --> 01:08:20.630
boy 17.

01:08:20.630 --> 01:08:21.880
Just consider it.

01:08:21.880 --> 01:08:25.210
And that to me is part
of why, not only are

01:08:25.210 --> 01:08:28.750
we talking about it in all these
rooms, but people are listening

01:08:28.750 --> 01:08:34.899
and we're being heard, which is
the next step to just listening

01:08:34.899 --> 01:08:36.819
because you politically
have to do it.

01:08:36.819 --> 01:08:37.569
Yes, that's right.

01:08:37.569 --> 01:08:43.930
I think what's interesting about
the 30% is that, for me, that

01:08:43.930 --> 01:08:46.359
feels like it's going
to take a long time,

01:08:46.359 --> 01:08:48.160
because we have to
have a pipeline.

01:08:48.160 --> 01:08:49.960
And these people have
to have experience.

01:08:49.960 --> 01:08:52.810
And we have to really build it.

01:08:52.810 --> 01:08:57.200
And that's hopefully
what's happening.

01:08:57.200 --> 01:08:58.729
Don't get caught
up on the pipeline.

01:08:58.729 --> 01:09:00.680
We built a studio
without knowing nothing

01:09:00.680 --> 01:09:01.899
about how to do it.

01:09:01.899 --> 01:09:03.859
And that's how Marvel
Studio was born.

01:09:03.859 --> 01:09:07.790
Please, don't get caught up on
the, we would-- no, just do it.

01:09:07.790 --> 01:09:09.189
Just do it, please.

01:09:09.189 --> 01:09:09.696
Do it.

01:09:09.696 --> 01:09:10.279
Get out there.

01:09:10.279 --> 01:09:11.779
I mean, I think we
are all doing it.

01:09:11.779 --> 01:09:14.401
I think everyone else
has to come on board.

01:09:14.401 --> 01:09:15.109
That's the thing.

01:09:15.109 --> 01:09:16.410
They have to do it.

01:09:16.410 --> 01:09:18.710
And I want to say that one
thing that LA Collab is

01:09:18.710 --> 01:09:20.149
during his meeting with studios.

01:09:20.149 --> 01:09:22.703
And they are looking.

01:09:22.703 --> 01:09:24.620
There are a lot of Latinos
that are qualified,

01:09:24.620 --> 01:09:26.450
guys, for these jobs, FYI.

01:09:26.450 --> 01:09:28.340
So I do believe in the
pipeline and all that.

01:09:28.340 --> 01:09:30.100
But really, there's a lot of us.

01:09:30.100 --> 01:09:33.590
And so what I'm seeing
is that a lot of them

01:09:33.590 --> 01:09:35.720
are open to having
this conversation.

01:09:35.720 --> 01:09:38.970
We are collecting resumes
and sending them their way.

01:09:38.970 --> 01:09:40.950
So there are a lot of
Latinos that are ready.

01:09:40.950 --> 01:09:43.663
So I think it's important.

01:09:43.663 --> 01:09:45.080
I had a conversation
with the head

01:09:45.080 --> 01:09:49.640
of a studio that reached out
for me to possibly come inside

01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:51.029
and leave my company.

01:09:51.029 --> 01:09:53.270
And one of the things--
he wanted to sit with me

01:09:53.270 --> 01:09:56.240
and ask me why certain
films didn't work.

01:09:56.240 --> 01:09:58.820
And when he brought up
this film and I told him,

01:09:58.820 --> 01:10:00.170
his face dropped.

01:10:00.170 --> 01:10:01.760
And he was shocked.

01:10:01.760 --> 01:10:04.728
He was absolutely shocked
at and why didn't anybody

01:10:04.728 --> 01:10:05.270
tell me that.

01:10:05.270 --> 01:10:08.780
And I said, because no one
looked like me in the room.

01:10:08.780 --> 01:10:10.760
And this is exactly what.

01:10:10.760 --> 01:10:12.780
You're making decisions
without us in the room.

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:14.900
It's just you're leaving
money on the table.

01:10:14.900 --> 01:10:16.290
It's really simple.

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:18.570
It's not fiscally
responsible, is it?

01:10:18.570 --> 01:10:20.350
No, it isn't.

01:10:20.350 --> 01:10:21.800
Yeah, it's silly.

01:10:21.800 --> 01:10:24.600
Well, mujeres,
thank you so much.

01:10:24.600 --> 01:10:26.080
This has been so incredible.

01:10:26.080 --> 01:10:28.910
I know we could sit here and
talk for hours and hours.

01:10:28.910 --> 01:10:31.910
And we have to do that at
some point once COVID ends.

01:10:31.910 --> 01:10:33.380
We'll do it in person.

01:10:33.380 --> 01:10:35.420
I just want to
thank you so much.

01:10:35.420 --> 01:10:37.220
What an amazing group of women.

01:10:37.220 --> 01:10:38.240
I'm so proud.

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:42.280
And I'm so happy
that you're all here.

01:10:42.280 --> 01:10:43.320
Hi, I'm Carmen Cuba.

01:10:43.320 --> 01:10:46.760
Thank you for watching
Academy Dialogues.

